=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR service unit (`frr.service`) and its `bgpd` daemon on `spine_router_0_0`.

**Mechanism:** FRR's `bgpd` is the process that maintains BGP TCP sessions (port 179) and exchanges UPDATE/KEEPALIVE messages with peers. When bgpd stops, every open TCP session it held is torn down immediately (TCP FIN/RST). All three configured BGP peers — `super_spine_router_0` (ASN 65000), `leaf_router_0_0` (ASN 65200), and `leaf_router_0_1` (ASN 65201) — each lost their session to spine_router_0_0 within seconds of each other because they were all terminated by the same single event: bgpd exiting. The peers transitioned to **Active** state (attempting to re-establish the TCP connection), but since bgpd is no longer listening, the sessions cannot come back up.

**Localization:** `spine_router_0_0` — `systemctl is-active frr` returns `inactive`; `pgrep bgpd` and `pgrep watchfrr` both return empty. Only `zebra`, `mgmtd`, and `staticd` remain as stand-alone processes. There is no BGP ASN configured in the active FRR state and zero neighbors are present. All four healthy routers (`spine_router_0_1`, both leaves, super-spine) have bgpd running normally; the failure is isolated to `spine_router_0_0`.

**Why hosts still communicate:** The redundant path through `spine_router_0_1` (all 3 sessions Established) continues to carry traffic, so the data plane is intact — only the fabric's redundancy is broken.

**Root cause:** The FRR service on `spine_router_0_0` was stopped or crashed, killing `bgpd` and `watchfrr`, and collapsing all three BGP sessions that spine_router_0_0 was maintaining.

**Fix:** `systemctl start frr` (or `systemctl restart frr`) on `spine_router_0_0` to bring bgpd back up; sessions will re-establish automatically once bgpd starts listening on port 179.
=== END ===